<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mindanao</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mindanao is the second-largest island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, in the south of the archipelago. Its western and southern parts were home to Muslim peoples and the Sultanate of Maguindanao, which, with the nearby Sulu Sultanate, resisted Spanish conquest for more than three centuries. Muslim regions of the island now form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangsamoro">Bangsamoro</a> Autonomous Region, created in 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mindanao.jpg" alt="Mindanao" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ESA Copernicus Sentinel-2, processed by EOX · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mindanao_Sentinel-2_MSI_2019_cloudless_composite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sulu Sultanate grew out of the trading chiefdoms of the Sulu archipelago, the chain of islands between <b>Mindanao</b> and northern Borneo inhabited by the Tausug and Sama peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It does not take into account the many other native house forms, such as those of the rulers of <b>Mindanao</b>, whose architecture shows strong Islamic influence.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bahay-kubo">Bahay Kubo</a></li><li><blockquote>Buayan was the upstream, sa raya, state of the Pulangi river basin in central <b>Mindanao</b>, while Maguindanao held the coast, sa ilud.</blockquote><a href="/state/buayan-sultanate">Buayan Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Muslim <b>Mindanao</b> and Sulu resisted conquest.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a settlement of the Bohol coast, probably on Panglao Island, ruled by datus and trading with other Visayan and <b>Mindanao</b> ports.</blockquote><a href="/state/kedatuan-of-dapitan">Kedatuan of Dapitan</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan Kudarat, reigning from about 1619 to 1671, united much of <b>Mindanao</b>, fought Governor Sebastian Hurtado de Corcuera in 1637 and made peace with Spain on his own terms.</blockquote><a href="/state/maguindanao-sultanate">Maguindanao Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
